description:
FILED s543 (the reviewing session, 2026-11-21) from a review probe of s543e; PRE-EXISTING (main e8dd2cdd and the s543e tree agree with each other, not with perl).

REPRODUCER:
    my $b = bless [1,2], "Pl";
    print +(sort { $b <=> $a } 3,1,2)[0], "\n";
  perl 5.40.3: 2, with the compile-time warning `"my $b" used in sort comparison`; PCL: 3 (the answer the programmer meant).

perl resolves `$b` in the block to the lexical in scope (a constant for the whole sort), so the comparator is inconsistent and the order is whatever the sort makes of it; PCL binds its comparator parameters over the name.  Nobody writes this on purpose -- it is the classic `my $a` / `my $b` trap -- and perl`s result is implementation-defined order.  Filed because it is a silent divergence; the honest fix may be an ANNOUNCEMENT at compile time (perl`s own warning text) rather than reproducing perl`s order.  Check `my $a` too.
